1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,705
You followed a Tetris tutorial to the end.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:08,130
Blocks fall, rotate, and disappear when a line fills.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,870
You typed the code yourself.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:13,305
But what happens when you close the project and

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,070
the tutorial, then open a new, empty project?

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,370
You may get stuck on the first line.

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:19,825
Where should I begin?

8
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,885
Today we will explore that gap: practicing the

9
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:25,525
move from understanding code when you see it

10
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:28,045
to starting a program yourself from nothing.

11
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:30,545
Even one moving block connects input,

12
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,410
position, collision, and state changes.

13
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:36,285
As we build, think about who decided how those

14
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,450
actions should happen and in what order.

15
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,265
I especially want to emphasize this to first-

16
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,590
and second-year computer science students.

17
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:48,125
While learning programming, turn off AI

18
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,345
and, when possible, start a program from

19
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,070
scratch without looking at the answer code.

20
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,190
The important question is what we mean by making it yourself.

21
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,825
Did your hands enter decisions made by someone

22
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:03,280
else, or did you decide how to solve the problem?

23
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:10,448
Typing a book example, reproducing an

24
00:01:10,463 --> 00:01:12,728
instructor’s function, and reading

25
00:01:12,743 --> 00:01:14,913
other people’s code all have a place.

26
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:17,873
When you are new, you may not even know where to begin.

27
00:01:18,063 --> 00:01:20,008
Here we read an existing node

28
00:01:20,023 --> 00:01:22,113
definition and insertion code.

29
00:01:22,263 --> 00:01:24,488
We find the variable that points to the next node,

30
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:27,553
then run the program to check the connections.

31
00:01:27,743 --> 00:01:30,328
This is time for learning the concept, and

32
00:01:30,343 --> 00:01:33,028
reading good code helps. Understanding this

33
00:01:33,043 --> 00:01:35,548
example alone does not yet establish that

34
00:01:35,563 --> 00:01:37,853
you can design the same feature yourself.

35
00:01:37,983 --> 00:01:41,013
Pause execution and follow the next pointer.

36
00:01:41,223 --> 00:01:42,848
Check which actual values

37
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:44,513
those names refer to.

38
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:46,828
After observing the principle, practice

39
00:01:46,843 --> 00:01:48,703
retrieving and using it on your own.

40
00:01:49,433 --> 00:01:52,863
I am not recommending that you abandon books and tutorials.

41
00:01:53,173 --> 00:01:55,598
Learn from them first, then set aside

42
00:01:55,613 --> 00:01:57,743
time to close the answer and rebuild.

43
00:01:58,013 --> 00:01:59,578
With the answer beside you, the

44
00:01:59,593 --> 00:02:01,543
next line is already visible.

45
00:02:01,693 --> 00:02:04,618
Starting from scratch requires you to decide which data

46
00:02:04,633 --> 00:02:07,203
you need and how to process it before writing that line.

47
00:02:07,373 --> 00:02:09,983
You do not need to memorize every language feature from the start.

48
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:12,598
Choose a small feature you have learned and see how far

49
00:02:12,613 --> 00:02:15,193
you can think through and implement it without help.

50
00:02:20,043 --> 00:02:22,688
Suppose you have studied linked lists: what

51
00:02:22,703 --> 00:02:25,853
a node is, how a pointer refers to the next

52
00:02:26,043 --> 00:02:28,513
node, and how insertion and deletion work.

53
00:02:28,663 --> 00:02:31,988
Now close the example and the tutorial. Temporarily turn

54
00:02:32,003 --> 00:02:35,568
off AI completion. Prepare your development environment

55
00:02:35,583 --> 00:02:38,833
and open a new project with an empty main function.

56
00:02:39,023 --> 00:02:41,373
Try implementing the linked list yourself.

57
00:02:41,483 --> 00:02:44,588
You might get stuck at how to declare a node.

58
00:02:44,603 --> 00:02:45,413
That is fine.

59
00:02:45,763 --> 00:02:47,308
You are finding where your

60
00:02:47,323 --> 00:02:49,593
independent implementation stops.

61
00:02:49,883 --> 00:02:52,228
Pause searches for the answer and

62
00:02:52,243 --> 00:02:54,513
write down the values a node needs.

63
00:02:55,003 --> 00:02:57,993
What should head contain before the first node exists?

64
00:02:58,143 --> 00:03:01,243
Decide the starting state of the empty list yourself.

65
00:03:01,967 --> 00:03:03,372
If you cannot write code

66
00:03:03,387 --> 00:03:05,337
yet, describe what you need.

67
00:03:05,447 --> 00:03:07,557
A node stores a value.

68
00:03:07,707 --> 00:03:09,877
A connection leads to the next node.

69
00:03:10,007 --> 00:03:12,617
A head pointer identifies the beginning.

70
00:03:12,787 --> 00:03:16,112
Start with a small goal: connect two nodes and print

71
00:03:16,127 --> 00:03:19,752
their values from the front. You do not have to finish

72
00:03:19,767 --> 00:03:22,757
insertion, deletion, and every edge case at once.

73
00:03:22,867 --> 00:03:24,912
That divides the goal into declaring

74
00:03:24,927 --> 00:03:27,417
nodes, linking them, and traversing them.

75
00:03:27,487 --> 00:03:30,272
When the first line will not come to mind,

76
00:03:30,287 --> 00:03:32,657
decide the first thing the program must do.

77
00:03:37,383 --> 00:03:40,508
In the editor, we create nodes

78
00:03:40,523 --> 00:03:43,268
containing ten and twenty. Typing

79
00:03:43,283 --> 00:03:46,508
speed is not the point; deciding

80
00:03:46,523 --> 00:03:49,473
what to store and where is.

81
00:03:49,643 --> 00:03:52,808
Make the first node point to the second

82
00:03:52,823 --> 00:03:55,533
and leave the final next pointer empty.

83
00:03:55,663 --> 00:03:58,928
Start at head and follow each connection while printing the values.

84
00:03:58,943 --> 00:04:02,613
Look at the actual output: does ten come before twenty?

85
00:04:02,763 --> 00:04:05,628
Thinking that the nodes are connected and checking

86
00:04:05,643 --> 00:04:08,533
that the code connects them are separate steps.

87
00:04:08,723 --> 00:04:10,628
Also check that traversal stops

88
00:04:10,643 --> 00:04:12,813
at the final empty connection.

89
00:04:13,023 --> 00:04:16,588
Confirm both the two printed values and the

90
00:04:16,603 --> 00:04:20,103
stopping condition before adding another feature.

91
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:24,023
Now we want to insert fifteen between ten and twenty.

92
00:04:24,193 --> 00:04:27,363
Creating a node alone does not put it into the list.

93
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:30,503
The connections must change too.

94
00:04:30,673 --> 00:04:32,818
First set the new node’s next pointer

95
00:04:32,833 --> 00:04:35,483
so we do not lose the existing twenty.

96
00:04:35,693 --> 00:04:38,683
Then make the preceding node point to the new node.

97
00:04:38,913 --> 00:04:42,138
Instead of memorizing the finished code, explain which connection

98
00:04:42,153 --> 00:04:44,918
to change first so the remaining nodes stay reachable.

99
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:46,913
Translate that explanation into code.

100
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:55,570
Back in the editor, implement insertion in the order we chose.

101
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,425
Run it and check that the values

102
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:00,630
appear as ten, fifteen, twenty.

103
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,485
If the output stops at fifteen and twenty

104
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:04,970
disappears, what should you suspect?

105
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:08,145
Check whether you lost the original next connection,

106
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:10,810
rather than only checking that the new node exists.

107
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:14,910
Pause at a breakpoint and inspect the next
pointers of the preceding and new nodes.

108
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:17,585
Compare what they point to

109
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,245
before and after insertion.

110
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:23,070
These changes correspond to the arrows we just saw.

111
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:25,165
Once twenty remains reachable,

112
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:27,810
insert another node.

113
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:31,810
Check that the order survives a different value.

114
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,120
Do not finish verification after a single successful output.

115
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:38,400
Delete the middle node.

116
00:05:38,550 --> 00:05:41,615
Make its predecessor point to the following

117
00:05:41,630 --> 00:05:44,580
node, then release the node you no longer use.

118
00:05:44,770 --> 00:05:47,840
Check that you never follow the deleted node again.

119
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:50,280
After middle deletion works, remove the first node.

120
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:52,380
What should head point to?

121
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:54,975
If the only node is deleted, how

122
00:05:54,990 --> 00:05:57,380
will you represent an empty list?

123
00:05:57,630 --> 00:06:00,220
Call the function on an empty list too.

124
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:03,115
If execution fails or accesses invalid

125
00:06:03,130 --> 00:06:05,835
memory, trace the missing condition.

126
00:06:05,850 --> 00:06:08,695
Change the inputs to check

127
00:06:08,710 --> 00:06:10,720
whether your rules actually work.

128
00:06:11,050 --> 00:06:13,135
After freeing a node, do

129
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:15,040
not read its value again.

130
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:17,335
Preserve the connection you need before

131
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:19,180
deletion and traverse only the surviving list.

132
00:06:19,490 --> 00:06:21,595
Observe the order of connection

133
00:06:21,610 --> 00:06:24,040
changes and cleanup in execution.

134
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:27,605
If declaring a node stops you, revisit how

135
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:29,905
to represent data. If insertion order stops

136
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:32,405
you, revisit connections. For deletion,

137
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:35,510
examine lifetime and boundary conditions.

138
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,425
Forgetting some code does not

139
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,550
mean all your study was wasted.

140
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:42,425
Independent implementation reveals more precisely

141
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:44,970
what you understand and where you need help.

142
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:47,750
After reviewing the concept, close the material and implement it again.

143
00:06:47,900 --> 00:06:50,085
Check whether you can explain why each line is

144
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:52,900
needed, rather than how many lines you memorized.

145
00:06:57,687 --> 00:06:59,053
Tetris works the same way.

146
00:06:59,243 --> 00:07:02,508
The instructor builds the board and blocks, implements

147
00:07:02,523 --> 00:07:06,113
collision and rotation, and adds line clearing.

148
00:07:06,263 --> 00:07:08,813
Following along produces a running game.

149
00:07:08,943 --> 00:07:10,413
Now try once more.

150
00:07:10,723 --> 00:07:12,993
Preserve and close the finished project.

151
00:07:13,103 --> 00:07:15,593
Turn off the tutorial and stop looking at its source.

152
00:07:15,843 --> 00:07:18,213
Open a new, empty project.

153
00:07:18,423 --> 00:07:22,308
The questions change: where should block data live? Should the

154
00:07:22,323 --> 00:07:26,368
board be a two-dimensional array? When should collision be

155
00:07:26,383 --> 00:07:30,213
checked? This time you must answer, rather than the instructor.

156
00:07:30,563 --> 00:07:33,193
Decide the first small version you can run.

157
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:35,468
Before scores and previews,

158
00:07:35,483 --> 00:07:37,973
display one block on the board.

159
00:07:38,123 --> 00:07:40,188
Start writing the necessary data in the new

160
00:07:40,203 --> 00:07:42,703
file and work toward that first execution.

161
00:07:43,433 --> 00:07:45,638
You do not have to pour out code for

162
00:07:45,653 --> 00:07:47,583
a complete Tetris game immediately.

163
00:07:47,793 --> 00:07:50,958
Divide the work into displaying the board and block,

164
00:07:50,973 --> 00:07:53,463
movement, collision, rotation, locking, and line clearing.

165
00:07:53,673 --> 00:07:56,378
Today, showing one block and moving

166
00:07:56,393 --> 00:07:58,963
it one cell right can be enough.

167
00:07:59,093 --> 00:08:02,323
Confirm that behavior, then prevent it from crossing the wall.

168
00:08:02,613 --> 00:08:06,023
Breaking up work and choosing its order are also programming practice.

169
00:08:06,073 --> 00:08:09,018
Before naming functions and classes, decide

170
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:11,753
which small result you can verify now.

171
00:08:16,450 --> 00:08:20,000
In the development view, first display an empty board.

172
00:08:20,210 --> 00:08:22,375
Keep the block shape separate from its

173
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:25,040
current position and draw a four-cell block.

174
00:08:25,230 --> 00:08:28,920
Connect the right-arrow input and move it one cell.

175
00:08:29,090 --> 00:08:31,675
Watch not only the block but also how

176
00:08:31,690 --> 00:08:34,240
its coordinates change after the input.

177
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:36,135
The displayed block begins to

178
00:08:36,150 --> 00:08:37,880
connect with the stored data.

179
00:08:37,990 --> 00:08:40,895
Instead of waiting for the instructor’s next line, you

180
00:08:40,910 --> 00:08:43,720
decide which value must change to create the behavior.

181
00:08:43,870 --> 00:08:45,535
Draw the same shape elsewhere.

182
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:48,475
If shape data and board position are separate, you

183
00:08:48,490 --> 00:08:51,800
can change position without rewriting the shape.

184
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:53,575
Check which tasks your

185
00:08:53,590 --> 00:08:55,490
representation makes easier.

186
00:08:56,217 --> 00:08:58,082
Changing the current position unconditionally on

187
00:08:58,097 --> 00:08:59,807
a right-arrow press lets the block cross a wall.

188
00:09:00,157 --> 00:09:02,787
Instead, first construct a candidate

189
00:09:02,937 --> 00:09:05,267
one cell to the right and check it.

190
00:09:05,577 --> 00:09:08,142
Check whether any occupied cell would leave

191
00:09:08,157 --> 00:09:10,387
the board or overlap an existing cell.

192
00:09:10,597 --> 00:09:12,967
Commit the position only when it is valid.

193
00:09:13,337 --> 00:09:14,862
The goal is not to memorize

194
00:09:14,877 --> 00:09:16,347
the correct move function.

195
00:09:16,517 --> 00:09:19,522
Build the decision sequence yourself: receive input,

196
00:09:19,537 --> 00:09:22,247
form a candidate, check it, and apply it if allowed.

197
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:30,197
After writing the movement function, do not test only the middle.

198
00:09:30,347 --> 00:09:33,437
Move to the right wall and press right again,

199
00:09:33,687 --> 00:09:36,517
then try the same input next to existing blocks.

200
00:09:36,827 --> 00:09:39,777
At a blocked position, verify that the coordinates remain unchanged.

201
00:09:39,947 --> 00:09:42,552
Even if the picture looks stationary, changed

202
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:45,197
internal values can break the next action.

203
00:09:45,627 --> 00:09:47,832
Compare movement in empty space

204
00:09:47,847 --> 00:09:49,897
with staying still when blocked.

205
00:09:50,267 --> 00:09:52,492
You are checking whether the actual input

206
00:09:52,507 --> 00:09:54,677
results match the rule you described.

207
00:09:55,067 --> 00:09:56,717
Now move downward.

208
00:09:56,767 --> 00:10:00,257
Can the same check work on that candidate position?

209
00:10:00,387 --> 00:10:03,612
Try another direction and confirm that it stops above the

210
00:10:03,627 --> 00:10:06,747
floor and occupied cells to see how far the rule applies.

211
00:10:07,467 --> 00:10:09,917
Rotation raises more questions.

212
00:10:10,167 --> 00:10:12,737
How should the shape be represented?

213
00:10:12,847 --> 00:10:15,317
How do we calculate the rotated cells?

214
00:10:15,487 --> 00:10:19,277
What if the new shape overlaps a wall or another block?

215
00:10:19,487 --> 00:10:21,872
For an initial exercise, choose a simple

216
00:10:21,887 --> 00:10:25,132
rule: check the rotated candidate and keep

217
00:10:25,147 --> 00:10:27,437
the original shape if it cannot be placed.

218
00:10:27,707 --> 00:10:29,892
You do not need every adjustment used by

219
00:10:29,907 --> 00:10:32,097
commercial Tetris rotation systems immediately.

220
00:10:32,347 --> 00:10:34,372
Begin by implementing and explaining

221
00:10:34,387 --> 00:10:36,507
your own small rule accurately.

222
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:45,190
Connect the function that constructs and checks a rotation candidate.

223
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:48,285
Rotate once in the middle, then press the

224
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:50,890
same key beside a wall and existing blocks.

225
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:53,505
Check that an invalid rotation is rejected

226
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,970
while the original shape and position remain.

227
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:58,910
Only a successful check should change the shape.

228
00:10:59,220 --> 00:11:02,065
If a bug appears, inspect the candidate result and commit

229
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:05,430
order before replacing the entire rotation formula.

230
00:11:05,700 --> 00:11:08,285
Separate a calculation error from

231
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:10,730
changing state despite a failed check.

232
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:13,450
Try another shape at the same position.

233
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:15,245
Is a rotation rejected when even one

234
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:17,290
of its four cells leaves the board?

235
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:20,885
Checking only the origin can differ

236
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:23,730
from checking every occupied cell.

237
00:11:24,483 --> 00:11:27,548
When a block cannot descend, lock it into the board, find

238
00:11:27,563 --> 00:11:30,953
full rows, remove them, and move the remaining rows down.

239
00:11:31,123 --> 00:11:34,213
One visible action contains that sequence.

240
00:11:34,403 --> 00:11:36,728
Instead of filling only one row, prepare

241
00:11:36,743 --> 00:11:39,453
a board that completes two adjacent rows.

242
00:11:39,723 --> 00:11:41,228
Check that removing one does not

243
00:11:41,243 --> 00:11:43,413
cause the next to be skipped.

244
00:11:43,623 --> 00:11:46,953
Decide which rows should remain and compare that expectation with the result.

245
00:11:47,203 --> 00:11:49,728
Go beyond saying the game ran once:

246
00:11:49,743 --> 00:11:52,828
record which conditions you checked.

247
00:11:52,843 --> 00:11:56,348
Leave a single block above the rows to be

248
00:11:56,363 --> 00:11:58,393
cleared and watch where it moves afterward.

249
00:11:58,983 --> 00:12:01,868
Verify that the surviving data reaches the position used by

250
00:12:01,883 --> 00:12:04,673
the next turn, not merely that the clearing effect plays.

251
00:12:05,850 --> 00:12:09,055
Reading AI output or a book example and

252
00:12:09,070 --> 00:12:11,780
understanding how it works is useful.

253
00:12:12,050 --> 00:12:15,740
Reading the structures other developers choose matters too.

254
00:12:15,970 --> 00:12:17,835
But reading alone mainly exercises

255
00:12:17,850 --> 00:12:20,140
recognizing something in front of you.

256
00:12:20,450 --> 00:12:23,055
Even after enough practice recognizing code, you

257
00:12:23,070 --> 00:12:25,400
still need separate practice producing it yourself.

258
00:12:25,550 --> 00:12:28,040
With an empty project, no answer is visible.

259
00:12:28,590 --> 00:12:32,195
Alongside recall, you need problem solving: dividing the task and

260
00:12:32,210 --> 00:12:35,835
choosing data structures, functions, and implementation order.

261
00:12:35,850 --> 00:12:38,640
Reading code can involve deep thought too.

262
00:12:38,870 --> 00:12:41,895
The point is to check comprehension and

263
00:12:41,910 --> 00:12:44,560
independent design and implementation separately.

264
00:12:49,583 --> 00:12:51,308
When rebuilding, try

265
00:12:51,323 --> 00:12:53,053
changing one condition.

266
00:12:53,243 --> 00:12:55,593
If you implemented insertion at the front

267
00:12:55,883 --> 00:12:58,253
of a linked list, try appending to the end.

268
00:12:58,403 --> 00:13:01,268
Here we insert the first node into an empty

269
00:13:01,283 --> 00:13:03,913
list, then append to a nonempty list.

270
00:13:04,163 --> 00:13:05,928
Decide how to handle head and the

271
00:13:05,943 --> 00:13:07,813
final connection in each case.

272
00:13:08,023 --> 00:13:09,728
Memorizing the old sequence of

273
00:13:09,743 --> 00:13:11,453
lines may leave you stuck again.

274
00:13:11,523 --> 00:13:14,088
Understanding the connections lets you

275
00:13:14,103 --> 00:13:16,528
explain and adapt the required operations.

276
00:13:16,543 --> 00:13:19,928
Front insertion changed head to the new node.

277
00:13:19,943 --> 00:13:22,873
Which connection changes for tail insertion?

278
00:13:23,283 --> 00:13:26,108
Traverse to the final node, attach a new

279
00:13:26,123 --> 00:13:29,173
value, and print the whole order again.

280
00:13:29,983 --> 00:13:33,208
Suppose you have read a thousand English sentences and

281
00:13:33,223 --> 00:13:36,708
can understand them. Close the book and try expressing

282
00:13:36,723 --> 00:13:39,893
your own thoughts aloud; suddenly nothing may come out.

283
00:13:40,023 --> 00:13:42,328
You need time both to understand what

284
00:13:42,343 --> 00:13:44,653
you see and to build sentences yourself.

285
00:13:44,843 --> 00:13:47,253
I think programming is similar.

286
00:13:47,383 --> 00:13:50,328
Reading linked-list code ten times is different from

287
00:13:50,343 --> 00:13:53,448
closing the answer and implementing it yourself.

288
00:13:53,463 --> 00:13:56,068
Understanding Tetris code and choosing its

289
00:13:56,083 --> 00:13:58,028
structure in an empty project are different too.

290
00:13:58,043 --> 00:13:59,148
Look at resources while learning.

291
00:13:59,163 --> 00:14:01,228
Read books, watch tutorials,

292
00:14:01,243 --> 00:14:03,413
and ask AI to explain concepts.

293
00:14:03,883 --> 00:14:05,448
Once you understand enough, close them and

294
00:14:05,463 --> 00:14:07,213
spend time making the program yourself.

295
00:14:12,033 --> 00:14:15,738
This time, describe the work briefly instead of reading

296
00:14:15,753 --> 00:14:19,838
a completed function: find full rows, decide what to

297
00:14:19,853 --> 00:14:23,463
remove, and gather the remaining rows at the bottom.

298
00:14:23,793 --> 00:14:26,858
Build a small function in that order, then

299
00:14:26,873 --> 00:14:29,923
run both a one-row and a two-row case.

300
00:14:30,193 --> 00:14:33,823
Pay attention to the input and the expected result together.

301
00:14:34,133 --> 00:14:37,258
Focus on whether your chosen processing order produces

302
00:14:37,273 --> 00:14:40,838
the result, rather than typing the same code repeatedly.

303
00:14:40,853 --> 00:14:43,758
An idea you could explain becomes

304
00:14:43,773 --> 00:14:45,703
conditions and iteration in code.

305
00:14:45,893 --> 00:14:48,378
Hide the function’s name and explain what

306
00:14:48,393 --> 00:14:50,923
input it receives and what result it produces.

307
00:14:51,213 --> 00:14:54,218
Check whether you understand the process or merely

308
00:14:54,233 --> 00:14:57,763
recognize the name, then try another input.

309
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:01,370
Starting from scratch naturally involves getting stuck.

310
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,545
You may forget a node declaration,

311
00:15:03,560 --> 00:15:05,945
confuse pointers, or wonder

312
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:08,010
how to check collisions.

313
00:15:08,300 --> 00:15:10,345
A problem that an answer solves in ten

314
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:12,785
seconds may take thirty minutes of

315
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:15,010
thought, or a bug may take two hours.

316
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:18,110
I believe that process can contain important practice.

317
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:21,325
Form an expectation, run the program, set a

318
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:24,745
breakpoint, inspect variables, and correct your

319
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:27,445
assumptions. Time spent stuck is not itself the

320
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:30,750
goal; what you checked and changed matters.

321
00:15:35,683 --> 00:15:38,733
Reproduce the block crossing the wall.

322
00:15:38,883 --> 00:15:42,208
Before changing random code, state the expectation: pressing

323
00:15:42,223 --> 00:15:45,593
right at the right wall should leave its position unchanged.

324
00:15:45,803 --> 00:15:48,828
Pause movement processing and inspect candidate

325
00:15:48,843 --> 00:15:51,513
coordinates, board width, and the collision result.

326
00:15:51,723 --> 00:15:54,028
Follow which cells are checked and

327
00:15:54,043 --> 00:15:56,128
when the position is committed.

328
00:15:56,143 --> 00:15:58,213
A bug can exist without an error message.

329
00:15:58,343 --> 00:16:00,928
Running successfully and following

330
00:16:00,943 --> 00:16:03,088
the intended rule are different.

331
00:16:03,103 --> 00:16:06,213
Find where the expectation first diverges from the result.

332
00:16:06,563 --> 00:16:09,788
Do not change several functions at once. Follow the values

333
00:16:09,803 --> 00:16:12,928
after this input reaches movement processing. Compare the

334
00:16:12,943 --> 00:16:15,988
current position before and after the check to distinguish

335
00:16:16,003 --> 00:16:18,333
forming a candidate from already changing the state.

336
00:16:19,533 --> 00:16:22,098
If the code changes position before

337
00:16:22,113 --> 00:16:24,943
checking collision, suspect the order.

338
00:16:25,133 --> 00:16:28,803
Modify it to check the candidate first and commit only on success.

339
00:16:29,053 --> 00:16:32,103
Press the same key beside the wall where the problem occurred.

340
00:16:32,373 --> 00:16:34,458
Confirm that position remains unchanged, and

341
00:16:34,473 --> 00:16:36,783
that movement still works in the empty center.

342
00:16:36,973 --> 00:16:40,203
Check that the fix did not block valid movement.

343
00:16:40,413 --> 00:16:43,558
Write one sentence explaining the cause: the current

344
00:16:43,573 --> 00:16:46,758
coordinate changed despite a failed check. That record

345
00:16:46,773 --> 00:16:49,683
gives you a starting point for the next investigation.

346
00:16:49,913 --> 00:16:51,723
Test the opposite wall too.

347
00:16:52,093 --> 00:16:54,678
Check whether the movement condition holds on both sides,

348
00:16:54,693 --> 00:16:57,023
rather than only whether the right-wall symptom disappeared.

349
00:16:57,393 --> 00:16:59,518
Record the inputs you

350
00:16:59,533 --> 00:17:01,943
retried alongside the fix.

351
00:17:02,733 --> 00:17:04,698
Thinking independently does not

352
00:17:04,713 --> 00:17:06,343
mean refusing help forever.

353
00:17:06,553 --> 00:17:09,098
If you keep circling the same problem, summarize the expected

354
00:17:09,113 --> 00:17:11,883
result, actual result, and what you have already checked.

355
00:17:12,033 --> 00:17:14,318
Ask a teacher or colleague, or consult the

356
00:17:14,333 --> 00:17:17,283
needed documentation and concept explanation.

357
00:17:17,673 --> 00:17:20,618
With AI, you can request a hint about the next

358
00:17:20,633 --> 00:17:23,343
condition to inspect instead of the entire solution.

359
00:17:23,473 --> 00:17:26,183
Once you understand the hint, close it and implement the idea.

360
00:17:26,393 --> 00:17:28,618
Explain why the change was needed and try

361
00:17:28,633 --> 00:17:31,158
applying it under similar conditions.

362
00:17:31,173 --> 00:17:33,623
Keep responsibility for the thinking.

363
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:41,485
If two rows should disappear but only one does,

364
00:17:41,500 --> 00:17:44,945
record that input and result before asking

365
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,930
for the whole Tetris game to be rewritten.

366
00:17:48,180 --> 00:17:51,670
Note how the row being checked changes during iteration.

367
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:53,805
After reviewing the concept, close the material

368
00:17:53,820 --> 00:17:56,530
and rewrite the small row-compaction function.

369
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,785
Test both adjacent and

370
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:00,285
separated full rows.

371
00:18:00,300 --> 00:18:02,745
Even after receiving help, make the

372
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:04,245
decisions, implement, and verify yourself.

373
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:07,325
Go beyond understanding the hint and check

374
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:09,570
whether the principle handles a changed input.

375
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:13,045
Record the missed condition: deleting a row changed

376
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:15,905
what would be checked next. Next time, decide which

377
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,270
rows remain and where they move first. Turn the

378
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:20,930
help into questions for your next implementation.

379
00:18:21,750 --> 00:18:25,695
That is why I recommend plenty of this practice in your first two years.

380
00:18:25,710 --> 00:18:29,320
After studying a data structure, close the answer and implement it.

381
00:18:29,550 --> 00:18:32,260
After studying an algorithm, close the explanation and solve it again.

382
00:18:32,590 --> 00:18:34,675
After a game programming tutorial, rebuild

383
00:18:34,690 --> 00:18:37,160
a small feature in an empty project.

384
00:18:37,570 --> 00:18:40,180
Before asking AI for finished code, take

385
00:18:40,410 --> 00:18:42,940
time to break up the problem yourself.

386
00:18:43,270 --> 00:18:46,315
As basic proficiency develops and projects

387
00:18:46,330 --> 00:18:49,115
grow in later years, use AI to improve

388
00:18:49,130 --> 00:18:52,195
productivity: repetitive code, library

389
00:18:52,210 --> 00:18:55,180
research, test ideas, and refactoring.

390
00:18:55,750 --> 00:18:58,500
Your year at university does not automatically determine your skill.

391
00:18:58,830 --> 00:19:01,255
Expand tool use based on whether you

392
00:19:01,270 --> 00:19:03,695
can choose the structure, verify

393
00:19:03,710 --> 00:19:05,910
results, and investigate problems.

394
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:13,925
Once you can explain the basic structure, you can

395
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:17,650
ask AI for repetitive setup code or draft tests.

396
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:20,165
Decide the inputs and expected

397
00:19:20,180 --> 00:19:22,530
results yourself first.

398
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:25,945
In the development view, read what the added code changes,

399
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:29,870
then retry an empty board, a wall, and a two-row clear.

400
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:32,770
Receiving a suggestion does not complete verification.

401
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:34,825
AI can then help implement work

402
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:36,465
you have defined more quickly.

403
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:39,625
If a result differs from your expectation, inspect

404
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:42,010
the program’s state and judge it yourself.

405
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:45,550
Passing tests does not replace checking the screen.

406
00:19:45,780 --> 00:19:48,465
Use actual inputs to move and rotate, and examine

407
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:51,310
whether the changed function affects other behavior.

408
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:54,590
Decide what to delegate and what to verify.

409
00:19:55,317 --> 00:19:57,062
My message is not that

410
00:19:57,077 --> 00:19:59,027
you should avoid AI.

411
00:19:59,277 --> 00:20:01,262
While learning programming, do not

412
00:20:01,277 --> 00:20:03,727
outsource the thinking process itself.

413
00:20:03,957 --> 00:20:07,502
At work, we consult documentation, read existing

414
00:20:07,517 --> 00:20:11,047
code, search, use AI, and ask colleagues.

415
00:20:11,237 --> 00:20:14,082
What matters is retaining the ability to turn a

416
00:20:14,097 --> 00:20:16,827
problem into a program while using those tools.

417
00:20:17,137 --> 00:20:18,962
Tools can be efficient when the goal

418
00:20:18,977 --> 00:20:20,922
is finishing an assignment quickly.

419
00:20:20,937 --> 00:20:24,022
When the goal is learning, I hope you will judge

420
00:20:24,037 --> 00:20:27,477
more than how fast the final result appeared.

421
00:20:32,167 --> 00:20:36,257
Now transfer the exercise to a small brick-breaker game.

422
00:20:36,427 --> 00:20:39,352
Display a ball and paddle, move the paddle with input,

423
00:20:39,367 --> 00:20:42,477
and decide which values change when the ball touches it.

424
00:20:42,687 --> 00:20:45,237
Do not simply paste Tetris code.

425
00:20:45,367 --> 00:20:47,672
Identify this game’s state and collision conditions,

426
00:20:47,687 --> 00:20:50,697
then implement and run one behavior at a time.

427
00:20:50,847 --> 00:20:53,197
Apply a learned principle to a new problem.

428
00:20:53,367 --> 00:20:54,912
You may be slow and get stuck at first.

429
00:20:54,927 --> 00:20:58,672
With practice, you begin to see what to

430
00:20:58,687 --> 00:21:02,597
store and in what order to process it.

431
00:21:02,767 --> 00:21:04,712
Test hits at the paddle’s

432
00:21:04,727 --> 00:21:06,777
center and edge.

433
00:21:07,007 --> 00:21:10,257
Write the expected response and compare it with the actual ball movement.

434
00:21:10,567 --> 00:21:12,532
Even in another game, narrowing problems by

435
00:21:12,547 --> 00:21:15,047
comparing predictions with execution remains useful.

436
00:21:15,767 --> 00:21:19,317
Practicing alone can leave you unsure where

437
00:21:19,367 --> 00:21:22,657
to begin or what to change in your code.

438
00:21:22,887 --> 00:21:25,112
Questions that organize your thoughts

439
00:21:25,127 --> 00:21:27,097
and feedback on your code can help.

440
00:21:27,387 --> 00:21:30,257
If you want to develop a habit of designing and implementing programs

441
00:21:30,487 --> 00:21:34,537
yourself, explore YamyamCoding programming coaching and tutoring.

442
00:21:34,787 --> 00:21:37,852
The information is for learners seeking direction in

443
00:21:37,867 --> 00:21:40,377
breaking down and implementing game programming problems.

444
00:21:40,687 --> 00:21:43,152
The coaching and tutoring link will be in the description,

445
00:21:43,167 --> 00:21:45,752
at the end of the video, and in the pinned comment.

446
00:21:45,767 --> 00:21:48,552
Summarize what you are learning, the code

447
00:21:48,567 --> 00:21:51,197
you have made, and where you are stuck.

448
00:21:51,567 --> 00:21:54,477
Begin with a specific account of the help you need.

449
00:21:59,417 --> 00:22:01,662
Choose just one small feature you have learned.

450
00:22:01,677 --> 00:22:05,082
Close the book and tutorial, temporarily

451
00:22:05,097 --> 00:22:08,507
turn off AI, and open an empty project.

452
00:22:08,697 --> 00:22:10,987
Start from nothing yourself.

453
00:22:11,117 --> 00:22:13,307
Your first attempt does not have to succeed.

454
00:22:13,457 --> 00:22:15,942
Find where you get stuck, learn what

455
00:22:15,957 --> 00:22:17,987
you need, and try making it again.

456
00:22:18,197 --> 00:22:20,782
After building your ability to decide and

457
00:22:20,797 --> 00:22:23,742
implement independently, use AI again as a tool

458
00:22:23,757 --> 00:22:26,407
that helps realize your own ideas much faster.

459
00:22:26,637 --> 00:22:30,202
Today’s first goal can be small: put a value into an empty

460
00:22:30,217 --> 00:22:34,282
list and take it out, or move and stop one object on

461
00:22:34,297 --> 00:22:37,887
screen. This time, choose that small beginning yourself.
